# SRG-APP-000033-WSR-000169  -  CAT II
# The web server must enforce approved authorizations for logical access to
# hosted applications and resources in accordance with applicable access
# control policies.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000033-WSR-000169
group:           V-206355
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000213
control_rev5:    AC-3

contribution:    Splunk Web resolves every authenticated identity to one or more roles
                 before serving content and refuses a request whose role lacks the
                 capability or the index scope for it, so the application's configurable
                 share of the authorization check is the role structure in authorize.conf
                 that the check is made against.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

retired_check:   authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] role_<roleName> present
                 reason: The stanza placeholder was repeated in the key position. The keys
                         of a role stanza are capabilities and search scope settings -
                         authorize.conf.spec:76 states them as "<capability> = enabled" -
                         so no key in a role stanza is named role_something, the condition
                         matched nothing, and it reported Open on every instance. That is a
                         wrong answer, not a strict one.

external:        Where identities come from an external store, the role a user receives is
                 decided by the group membership that authentication.conf
                 [roleMap_<authSettings-key>] (authentication.conf.spec:351) or
                 [roleMap_<saml-authSettings-key>] (:1230) maps; the directory service or
                 identity provider asserts that membership and is assessed under its own
                 baseline.
external:        Whether the role an identity receives is the correct one for the content
                 requested is fixed by the organization's access control policy, not by any
                 Splunk setting.

manual_reason:   The benchmark check text asks whether an authorization check is performed
                 before the requested content is served. Splunk Web is the product's web
                 management interface, served on port 8000 by default. No setting in the
                 specification set shipped with this version turns that check on or off, so
                 no configuration value decides the requirement. Treating the check as
                 always performed would be an inherent classification, which needs its own
                 evidence and is not asserted here. The role structure the check is made
                 against is reported in the evidence, and whether the role an identity
                 holds is the right one for the content requested is fixed by the access
                 control policy. A condition testing only that roles exist cannot fail,
                 because the product ships role stanzas in system/default, so it would be
                 inert - the same objection recorded below against enableCertBasedUserAuth.

citation:        CHANGED for INVENTION. The condition 'web.conf [settings]
                 enableCertBasedUserAuth present' has been removed. Per
                 web.conf.spec:120-131 that setting selects whether splunkd uses a digital
                 certificate to identify a user, and :125 requires requireClientCert to be
                 true alongside it; it governs how an identity is established, not whether
                 an established identity is authorized for the content it requested, which
                 is the whole of this check text. The operand appears in neither the check
                 text nor the fix text, and no product STIG rule shares this CCI. It is
                 also inert as a test: the setting has a default of false
                 (web.conf.spec:131), so in the merged configuration the assessment reads
                 it is always present and the condition can never fail, which leaves a
                 reader of the checklist with certificate-based authentication cited as the
                 criterion for an authorization requirement and no evidence behind it.
                 certBasedUserAuthMethod and requireClientCert are left out for the same
                 reason, with the role mapping that external providers drive recorded in
                 external_dependencies. The wildcard-stanza evaluation rule given under
                 SRG-APP-000033-AU-001610 applies to the remaining condition: it is
                 satisfied when at least one [role_<roleName>] stanza is present.
                 That remaining condition is itself now retired, above, and this
                 requirement is decided as manual, for the reason given under
                 SRG-APP-000033-AU-001610: the condition repeated the stanza placeholder in
                 the key position and so reported Open on every instance, and the existence
                 test it was meant to be cannot fail.
finding:         Not a finding against the application: Splunk Web resolves an
                 authenticated identity to its roles before serving content and refuses a
                 request whose role lacks the capability or the index scope for it. Whether
                 the role an identity holds is the correct one for the content requested is
                 decided against the organization's access control policy, which is an
                 assessor inspection.
